# Rank 1648 / CVE-2022-23517: rails-html-sanitizer's permit scrubber rewrote the
# SVG attributes that may carry a local reference with an unanchored global
# regex substitution, and the substitution's cost is quadratic in the length of
# the attacker-supplied attribute value (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression
# complexity, CWE-400; GHSA-5x79-w82f-gw8w).
#
# `PermitScrubber#scrub_attribute` (lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb:148-150 at the
# fix parent) reaches, for every attribute named in
# `Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF` (`fill`, `stroke`,
# `filter`, `clip-path` and the rest),
# `attr_node.value = attr_node.value.gsub(/url\s*\(\s*[^#\s][^)]+?\)/m, " ")`.
# The pattern is unanchored and applied globally, so `gsub` retries it at every
# `url(` in the attribute value, and each retry grows `[^)]+?` one character at
# a time to the end of the value when no `)` follows -- a value built out of
# repeated `url(` prefixes with no closing parenthesis costs O(n^2). The
# attribute value is markup the caller handed the sanitizer, which is the input
# a sanitizer exists to accept, so the bound is entirely the attacker's.
#
# The pool row carries no flag, and 56c61c0cebd1e493e8ad7bca2a0191609a4a6979
# is still read before being trusted: it is the real, targeted fix ("fix:
# replace slow regex attribute check with Loofah method"), neither a merge nor
# a release bump, it touches lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb plus a regression
# test, and the remediation is present at the commit itself. The `gsub` call is
# replaced by `Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.scrub_attribute_that_allows_local_ref`,
# which Loofah implements with `Crass::Parser#parse_component_values`, keeping
# only `:url` component values whose target starts with `#` along with `:hash`,
# `:ident` and `:string` values.orrection is needed.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, and the two absences are the ones recorded for
# the loofah copy of this same regex, re-measured here on the
# rails-html-sanitizer tree rather than inherited.
#
# First, the pattern is not classified as backtracking-prone, and that
# classification is correct as far as it goes: `regexambig.Ambiguous` answers
# false for `url\s*\(\s*[^#\s][^)]+?\)` because the pattern has no overlapping
# quantified branches -- `\s*` is followed by `\(` and by `[^#\s]`, both
# disjoint from `\s`, and `[^)]+?` is followed by `\)`, which it excludes. The
# cost is polynomial in the number of start positions `gsub` retries, not
# exponential in one match attempt, and polynomial backtracking is a class the
# engine does not model. So the `regex` node lowers to a plain `nir.Const`
# instead of the `__regex.match` call that `rubyRegexMayBacktrack`
# (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/ruby.go:1152) synthesises for a
# backtracking-prone literal, and no `code.RegexMatch`, `code.RegexMatchSink`
# or `code.CatastrophicRegex` label is ever emitted: `regexMatch205` in
# vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql keys on that synthesised node.
# `vyql match` over a repro of the real method reports no source and no sink
# whatsoever; the single label is `threat.CharFilter` on
# `attr_node.value.gsub`. A bounded scan of the real checkout at the vulnerable revision
# (`--cache off --max-ram 6GB`) reports 0 findings over ruby:6.
#
# Second, there is no source side either. `vyql/bindings/ruby/` carries no
# Nokogiri or Loofah binding (a grep for nokogiri, loofah and crass over the
# Ruby bindings finds nothing), so nothing labels a parsed attribute's value as
# untrusted markup. `vyql resolve` over the repro reports
# `Loofah.HTML5.SafeList.SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF.include?` unresolved, and the
# attribute iteration has no source label independently of the sink half.
# VYQL-DOS-002 asks for `core.UserControlledData` to reach
# `code.RegexMatchSink` and neither endpoint exists.
#
# Third, and specific to this revision's call form: even a pattern the engine
# does classify cannot report through `gsub`. When `rubyRegexMayBacktrack`
# answers true, the `regex` node lowers to an `__regex.match` call whose only
# argument is the pattern text itself (ruby.go:1068), so a regex supplied as a
# call argument -- `value.gsub(/re/, " ")`, the dominant sanitizer idiom --
# produces a sink with no link to the receiver it is applied to. The one route
# that links an operand is `case value when /re/`, which `rubyCase` lowers to a
# Switch carrying the case value as Subject and the regex as a Label. Measured
# with the isolating pair below: the same nested-ambiguous-quantifier pattern
# over the same parameter reports VYQL-DOS-002 in the `case`/`when` form and
# reports nothing as a `gsub` argument. So this revision needs both the cost
# model and the call-argument operand link before any binding could reach it.
#
# Teaching the classifier polynomial backtracking is what a fix would mean, and
# the measurement that killed it stands: over the `lib/` trees of loofah, rack
# and sanitize -- 268 Ruby regex literals, all three libraries whose job is
# handling untrusted input -- every candidate pattern shape flags 1 real in 7
# to 11, and rack's `/bytes=([^;]+)/` against the untrusted `Range` header is
# structurally identical to this pattern and harmless. The discriminator is not
# in the pattern; it is that the operand is an unbounded attacker-supplied
# attribute value, and what the fixed revision changes is the algorithm that reads it.
# Keying instead on the Loofah call the fixed revix installs would name the fixing
# commit's own vocabulary and detect nothing without the missing endpoints.
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1648: the permit scrubber substitutes the SVG ref attributes with a quadratic regex"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  require "cgi"

  module Rails
    module Html
      class PermitScrubber < Loofah::Scrubber
        def scrub_attributes(node)
          node.attribute_nodes.each do |attr_node|
            scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          end
        end

        def scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          attr_name = if attr_node.namespace
                        "#{attr_node.namespace.prefix}:#{attr_node.node_name}"
                      else
                        attr_node.node_name
                      end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ATTR_VAL_IS_URI.include?(attr_name)
            val_unescaped = CGI.unescapeHTML(attr_node.value).gsub(Loofah::HTML5::Scrub::CONTROL_CHARACTERS, "").downcase
            if val_unescaped =~ /^[a-z0-9][-+.a-z0-9]*:/ && !Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ALLOWED_PROTOCOLS.include?(val_unescaped.split(Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::PROTOCOL_SEPARATOR)[0])
              attr_node.remove
            end
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF.include?(attr_name)
            attr_node.value = attr_node.value.gsub(/url\s*\(\s*[^#\s][^)]+?\)/m, " ") if attr_node.value
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ALLOW_LOCAL_HREF.include?(node.name) && attr_name == "xlink:href" && attr_node.value =~ /^\s*[^#\s].*/m
            attr_node.remove
          end

          Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.force_correct_attribute_escaping! node
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1648: the permit scrubber handing the attribute to Loofah's component-value parser is clean"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  require "cgi"

  module Rails
    module Html
      class PermitScrubber < Loofah::Scrubber
        def scrub_attributes(node)
          node.attribute_nodes.each do |attr_node|
            scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          end
        end

        def scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          attr_name = if attr_node.namespace
                        "#{attr_node.namespace.prefix}:#{attr_node.node_name}"
                      else
                        attr_node.node_name
                      end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ATTR_VAL_IS_URI.include?(attr_name)
            val_unescaped = CGI.unescapeHTML(attr_node.value).gsub(Loofah::HTML5::Scrub::CONTROL_CHARACTERS, "").downcase
            if val_unescaped =~ /^[a-z0-9][-+.a-z0-9]*:/ && !Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ALLOWED_PROTOCOLS.include?(val_unescaped.split(Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::PROTOCOL_SEPARATOR)[0])
              attr_node.remove
            end
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF.include?(attr_name)
            Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.scrub_attribute_that_allows_local_ref(attr_node)
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ALLOW_LOCAL_HREF.include?(node.name) && attr_name == "xlink:href" && attr_node.value =~ /^\s*[^#\s].*/m
            attr_node.remove
          end

          Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.force_correct_attribute_escaping! node
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

# The isolating pair, through the `gsub` call form this revision actually uses.
# Same class shape, same parameter, no library API. Two axes are isolated:
# the quantifier structure of the literal, and the call form carrying it. The
# `case`/`when` form is the one route that links an operand to the pattern --
# `rubyCase` lowers it to a Switch whose Subject is the case value and whose
# Labels include the regex -- so the reported case below proves the classifier
# and the source both work when the link exists, and the third block proves the
# link is what the `gsub` form lacks: the same reportable pattern, an argument
# of a call, lowers to an `__regex.match` node whose only argument is the
# pattern text (`ruby.go:1068`), so no operand can reach it and VYQL-DOS-002
# cannot connect even for a pattern it does classify.

test "rank1648 pair: a nested ambiguous quantifier in the url() pattern is reported"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  class LocalRefScrubber
    def call(value)
      case value
      when /url\s*\(\s*([^#\s]+)+\)/m
        ""
      else
        value
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1648 pair: the quadratic url() pattern in the same shape is not reported"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
